WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.199
- If you forgive the
poor attempt at Irish,

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:06.650
(speaks Gaelic) I'm at home.

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:07.560
(audience clapping)

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.150
- [Narrator] President
Biden clearly takes pride

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.070
in his Irish heritage.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.440
- As long as I can remember it's been,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.090
sort of, part of my soul.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.770
- [Narrator] He enjoys
dropping old Irish sayings

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:17.603
into conversation.

00:00:17.603 --> 00:00:20.250
- If you're lucky enough to be Irish,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.800
well you're lucky enough.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.780
- [Narrator] And when presented
with a Mongolian horse,

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.560
named the animal Celtic.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.130
Now, Biden has visited his ancestral home

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.690
for the first time as president

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.060
in what was both an
important personal pilgrimage

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.613
and a demonstration of
American soft power abroad.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.910
The second Catholic
president's visit to Ireland

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.810
came 60 years after the
first, John F. Kennedy.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.690
- I want to say that I'm
happy to be here tonight.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:46.890
- [Narrator] And
continued a long tradition

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.930
of US leaders gracing the Emerald Isle.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.620
- I want you to know that for
this great grandson of Ireland

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.150
this is a moment of joy.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.583
- Many American presidents
have described their days

00:00:56.583 --> 00:00:59.100
either visiting Belfast or Dublin

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.410
as some of the best days
of their presidency.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.240
Bill Clinton visited three times.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.400
George W. Bush visited twice.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.140
Barack Obama visited once.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.010
And now Biden, who claims
a lot of Irish ancestry.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.030
- [Narrator] During his trip,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.700
Biden visited the place his
mother's family came from,

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.030
met the Irish president and
addressed parliament in Dublin.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.070
- The United States was shaped by Ireland

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.330
and that's not hyperbole.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.160
- [Narrator] The influence
of Biden's Irish roots

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.380
has long been seen in
his political rhetoric.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.460
His speeches, for example,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.800
often make references to Irish poetry.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.210
- You know, as the great Irish poet

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.567
Seamus Heaney once wrote,

00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:34.140
"History says, Don't hope

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.760
On this side of the grave,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.115
But then, once in a lifetime

00:01:38.115 --> 00:01:40.200
The longed-for tidal wave

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.060
Of justice rises up,

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.373
And hope and history rhyme"

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.430
It's everything I've been taught.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.450
- I think for President Biden,
the experience of being Irish

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.866
and the historical experience of

00:01:53.866 --> 00:01:56.190
Irish living under British rule

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.560
is a way that he uses to sort of connect

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.380
with oppressed minorities
in other parts of the world.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.500
So he's made it a real part
of his political worldview.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.530
- My background and the
background of my family

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:12.530
is Irish American and
we have a long history

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:21.180
of not fundamentally unlike
the Palestinian people

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.560
with Great Britain

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.520
and their attitude toward
Irish Catholics over the years.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:27.600
- [Narrator] Biden's Irish connection

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.460
has helped strengthen already strong links

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.350
between the US and Ireland.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.150
- I think it's fair to say that today

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.490
we're celebrating our national day

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.654
with a president who is
unmistakably a son of Ireland.

00:02:38.654 --> 00:02:41.130
(audience clapping)

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.990
- [Narrator] Being a son
of Ireland has also got him

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.940
out of one or two verbal gaps in the past.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:45.773
- He knows lot about it.

00:02:45.773 --> 00:02:50.223
His mom lived in Long
Island for 10 years or so.

00:02:50.223 --> 00:02:51.990
God rest her soul.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.440
And although she's,
wait, your mom's still,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.230
your mom's still alive, your dad passed.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:02:59.569
God bless her soul.

00:02:59.569 --> 00:03:00.450
(audience laughing)

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.660
There's an old Irish
proverb, as some of you know,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.490
that I heard my grandfather use

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.260
but never really applied to me before.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:12.018
He said, it goes, "A silent
mouth is sweet to hear."

00:03:12.018 --> 00:03:13.410
(audience laughing)

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.571
Well, I'm gonna yield to that proverb.

00:03:15.571 --> 00:03:16.404
(audience laughing)

00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:19.620
- I think the Irish in
general are very pro-American.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.540
I mean, the connections are obviously huge

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.586
because so many of its
sons and daughters left

00:03:24.586 --> 00:03:26.194
and many of them went to America

00:03:26.194 --> 00:03:28.410
and made something of themselves.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:32.250
So I think it's nice to have
some of these people return,

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.340
create new links between
Ireland and America.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.140
- [Narrator] This is
particularly important now,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.590
25 years since the Good
Friday Agreement was signed.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.170
The landmark peace deal between
Republicans and Unionists

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.200
in Northern Ireland was
negotiated with US help.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.250
- I think for America,

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:50.700
it sort of brings a reminder
of the times in the world

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.890
when American hard power and soft power

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.110
can sort of make a difference for good.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.264
- [Narrator] The President
had the opportunity

00:03:56.264 --> 00:03:58.620
during his visit to push
for its preservation

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.050
amid political instability in the region.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:02.430
- As a friend,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.070
I hope it's not too
presumptuous of me to say

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.680
that I believe democratic institutions

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.720
establishing the Good Friday Agreement

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.450
remain critical to the
future of Northern Ireland.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:16.440
- The Good Friday Agreement is
an unquestioned win for peace

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.240
and America was a big part of that.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.450
So I think Biden's going to
rightly sort of celebrate that.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.610
- Your history is our history.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.890
But even more important, your
future is America's future.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.473
(bouncy music)

